{"task_id": "HumanEval/119", "solution": "def match_parens(lst):\n    '''\n    You are given a list of two strings, both strings consist of open\n    parentheses '(' or close parentheses ')' only.\n    Your job is to check if it is possible to concatenate the two strings in\n    some order, that the resulting string will be good.\n    A string S is considered to be good if and only if all parentheses in S\n    are balanced. For example: the string '(())()' is good, while the string\n    '())' is not.\n    Return 'Yes' if there's a way to make a good string, and return 'No' otherwise.\n    '''\n\n    def _get_balance_info(s: str) -> tuple[int, int]:\n        # Calculates the final and minimum balance for a single parentheses string.\n        balance = 0\n        min_balance = 0\n        for char in s:\n            if char == '(':\n                balance += 1\n            else:  # char == ')'\n                balance -= 1\n            min_balance = min(min_balance, balance)\n        return balance, min_balance\n\n    def _can_form_balanced_concatenation(s1: str, s2: str) -> bool:\n        # Determines if concatenating s1 then s2 forms a balanced string.\n        final_balance_s1, min_balance_s1 = _get_balance_info(s1)\n        final_balance_s2, min_balance_s2 = _get_balance_info(s2)\n\n        # A balanced string must have a total balance of zero and\n        # never drop below zero at any point during parsing.\n        if final_balance_s1 + final_balance_s2 != 0:\n            return False\n        if min_balance_s1 < 0:\n            return False\n        if min_balance_s2 + final_balance_s1 < 0:\n            return False\n\n        return True\n\n    s1, s2 = lst[0], lst[1]\n\n    # Check if s1 followed by s2 forms a good string.\n    if _can_form_balanced_concatenation(s1, s2):\n        return 'Yes'\n\n    # Check if s2 followed by s1 forms a good string.\n    if _can_form_balanced_concatenation(s2, s1):\n        return 'Yes'\n\n    return 'No'"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/122", "solution": "def add_elements(arr, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a non-empty array of integers arr and an integer k, return\n    the sum of the elements with at most two digits from the first k elements of arr.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for i in range(k):\n        element = arr[i]\n        if -99 <= element <= 99:\n            total_sum += element\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "def get_odd_collatz(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer `n`, return a sorted list of all odd numbers in its Collatz sequence.\n    The list includes `1` and is sorted in increasing order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        return [1]\n\n    odd_numbers = set()\n    current = n\n    while current != 1:\n        if current % 2 != 0:\n            odd_numbers.add(current)\n\n        if current % 2 == 0:\n            current //= 2\n        else:\n            current = (3 * current) + 1\n\n    odd_numbers.add(1)\n\n    return sorted(list(odd_numbers))"}
